How Radiation Affects Heart Tissue
When radiation passes through your body, it doesn't just disappear. It interacts with cells, including those in your heart. Even at low levels, this exposure can damage the delicate lining of blood vessels. Think of it like tiny scratches building up over years.
The heart's electrical system can also be affected. Low-level radiation may alter how cells communicate, leading to irregular rhythms or other issues down the line. This doesn't happen overnight. The damage accumulates slowly, which is why it often goes unnoticed until symptoms appear.
Medical professionals who specialize in cardiac care study these patterns. They understand that repeated exposure, even in small amounts, can contribute to inflammation in heart tissue. This inflammation is linked to various cardiovascular problems, from coronary artery disease to heart failure.
Who's Most at Risk?
Not everyone faces the same level of risk. Medical workers, especially those in radiology or cardiology departments, have higher exposure. Pilots and flight attendants also experience more radiation due to altitude. But the biggest source for most people? Medical imaging.
CT scans, fluoroscopy, and cardiac catheterization all use radiation. If you've had multiple procedures, your cumulative dose adds up. This is where talking to a heart specialist becomes important. They can assess whether your exposure history warrants additional monitoring.
